In a glade where the rivers sang, there lived a clockmaker named Liora. Her hands were steady as a drumbeat, and she could coax time from the gears of any object. People brought her broken moments—an hourglass that forgot how to spill, a pocket watch that skipped birthdays, a sun-dial with a cranky shadow. Liora mended them with patient chants and threads spun from dawn.

One dusk, a traveler arrived, cloak stitched from night rain. He carried a silent compass that never pointed north, for its needle desired stories more than destinations. He asked Liora to fix it, promising a toll not of coins but of memories. “Return to me a memory I have not yet lived,” he said, “and I shall give you a memory you have never forgotten.”

Liora worked as the stars pressed closer. She opened the traveler’s chest to reveal a single seed, dark as a closed eye. She planted it in the garden where time slept beneath the roots of an old oak. Seasons turned in a heartbeat; the seed sprouted into a small tree whose fruit was a single moment: a memory you could pluck, taste, and set back into your heart.

The traveler returned, the compass finally naming a path, and Liora learned that the worth of fixing time wasn’t in hours saved but in the sweetness of the moments we choose to remember.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

